00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:43.230
in her introductory speech, her statement.

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:48.190
And so, I want to sum up hundreds of years

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:54.110
of research and science
in three simple messages.

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:55.290
You see, right?

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:59.580
Hundreds of years, yes, in three messages

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:04.580
and then I'm going to tell you
how also this is translated

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:11.040
for the humanitarian
community, so the ones,

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:13.290
the humanitarian workers
like me who are working

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:16.170
in the field in many countries,

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:21.030
responding to conflict
or to natural disaster,

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:25.170
how we transform all these
messages into commitments

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:28.080
for young children in the field, right?

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:31.230
And then I think we'll have
a space in the weeks to come

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:32.910
or in the months to come to look

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:36.000
at these very specific
examples of programs

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:40.380
and of all the regions
and context of the world.

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:45.380
So first of all, science, and very simply,

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:48.930
what I aspire to do with
this little introduction is

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:50.073
to change your lives.

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:54.270
I am aspiring to change the
way you are looking at life,

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.430
the way you are looking at yourself,

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.530
the way you are looking at your nephews

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.810
or your little nieces or your children

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:03.750
or your grandchildren or your neighbors.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.880
So my three messages are the following.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:07.570
The first one is

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:14.300
that genetics and environment
are very much linked

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:18.240
and now it's something so clear to us.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:21.510
Our DNA is not our destiny.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:24.850
We are going to develop and to unlock

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:30.330
and fold the different skills
and capacities in relation

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:33.000
to the environment that is around us.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.610
And there is an element that
is quite interesting now,

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:37.053
what we have discovered.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:39.600
We already guessed it before,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:44.600
but now we have a very
clear evidence for that,

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:48.690
that we human beings as mammals,

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:53.690
we are very social and
we need love and peace

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:55.770
to develop healthy and

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:59.460
to become the best versions of ourselves,

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:02.640
if I can say it that way.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:05.130
In a way, I'm not going
to talk about epigenetics.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:10.130
We'll have time for more,

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:13.350
you know, engagement and discussions

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:15.750
but that will be the first message.

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:19.200
The second message is that time matters.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:20.970
And in my slide I put there,

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:23.280
I did the reference to
the Bucharest study,

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:24.750
which is for me,

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:26.670
it has been one of the
most interesting studies

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:28.983
on early childhood development.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.640
What do I mean by time matters?

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:36.000
So what happens in the brain
of a child is the following.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:39.723
When we are in the wombs of our mothers,

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.790
the neurons starts developing, creating

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:46.290
in the wombs of our mothers

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:49.840
and I think there are
some medicine students

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:52.650
among the distinguished participants

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:57.650
who will be much more articulated
than I am to explain that

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:00.183
in terms of numbers.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:05.470
But when we are born
with around 100 million,

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.340
100 billion neurons, is that correct Jim?

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:14.343
I look at Jim to go for
confirmation. Almost.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:17.820
The connectivity of those cells,

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:22.820
the synapses is not that big,

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:27.860
but it accelerates in a crazy
way through the engagement

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:32.550
with the environment, to the
age of three, more or less,

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.640
those first 1,000 days of life.

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.340
The multiplication of this synapses,

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:42.630
this electric connectivity
in the brain, goes insane.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:45.030
Well, it's not insane. It's
actually something very sane.

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:50.030
But it's very, very, very
strong, at the speed of

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:54.120
around one million new neuronal
connections per second.

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:59.010
And from there, in the
second 1001st of life,

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:02.340
there is a very close
pruning of the brain,

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:03.450
of the child, right?

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:05.760
Pruning meaning there is some

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:09.240
of those connections
disappeared 'cause the brain

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:12.510
of a child becomes very, very specialized.

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:16.110
What it means, this very
early period of life,

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:18.510
is that we have a unique,

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:23.430
incredible window of opportunity
to set up who we are.

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:26.760
And maybe you don't remember
those years of your life,

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.370
but that was the most formative,

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:34.370
essential, key moments of your life

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:38.370
that created who you are, right,

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:42.750
this stable opportunity in these few years

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:45.870
to set up empathetic
human beings who are going

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:48.420
to be social, who are
going to be more engaged,

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:53.130
who are going to be
healthier, more skilled.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:57.000
So all the potential that
we have when we are born,

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.460
we have that unique window of opportunity

00:22:02.896 --> 00:22:06.800
to support this healthy, happy development

00:22:08.225 --> 00:22:12.570
and create the peaceful human
beings who are the elements

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:14.790
of the peaceful communities,
the peaceful families

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:17.820
and the peaceful communities
and the peaceful nations

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:20.160
that we all aspire for.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:21.330
I am not a determinist.

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:25.080
That doesn't mean that we
cannot do things afterwards.

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:26.610
Of course we are. (chuckles)

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.437
We are working with
adolescents and with children

00:22:28.437 --> 00:22:30.235
and with adults and with everyone,

00:22:30.235 --> 00:22:32.493
the plasticity of the brain,

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.870
even if it is incredible in those years

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:40.830
but it exists until we disappear.

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:44.688
My third message is toxic stress,

00:22:44.688 --> 00:22:49.350
what our colleagues in the
area are now call toxic stress.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:53.110
I mentioned toxic stress,
obviously very related

00:22:55.025 --> 00:23:00.000
to difficult situations at
home, but also in communities.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.520
Rima has given us very compelling data

00:23:04.685 --> 00:23:08.427
on children living in
situations of conflict,

00:23:08.427 --> 00:23:11.460
1.6 billion children living in countries

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:13.080
where there is conflict.

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:15.450
What happens when there is toxic stress?

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:18.570
What has happened when we
have a continuous response

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:23.407
of the systems in the body
that responds to that stress?

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:32.220
What happens is there is a risk.

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:37.220
There is a risk of derailment,
of healthy development

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:42.410
and that is not a small
matter because particularly

00:23:44.310 --> 00:23:47.670
in situations of emergency or in families

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:52.657
where there is a lot of stress
that turns into toxic stress,

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:59.700
we are risking the development of children

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:03.540
and the consolidation of the creation

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:07.050
of the formation of peaceful
families who are the selves

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.420
of peaceful societies.

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:11.370
So three simple messages,

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:14.760
and how do we translate these into action?

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:17.457
And we are going to see that much more

00:24:17.457 --> 00:24:20.370
and we have to really explore
much more how we are going

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:23.160
to translate all the science
and all the knowledge

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:25.620
that we have in these
incredible universities

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:27.300
and these amazing academics

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.700
that have been studying
for many, many years,

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:32.700
also obviously in partnership
with practitioners,

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:34.530
et cetera, but how do we translate

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:35.910
of this knowledge into action

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:39.213
so we really build those
more peaceful societies?

00:24:41.615 --> 00:24:44.220
How can all this science help us

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:49.220
to work effectively and
efficiently in the field,

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:53.430
in the most difficult environments,

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:55.920
in the most difficult
communities and in countries

00:24:55.920 --> 00:25:00.070
where there is conflict or
where there is emergencies

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:04.020
and natural disasters that
create a lot of stress

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.763
for families and young children?

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:11.840
So we have recently made
this much more official

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:18.333
to say when there is an emergency,

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:22.770
the ones who are working in emergencies

00:25:23.957 --> 00:25:27.810
with all the nations
and every woman and man

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:32.810
of goodwill in the world, we
are committing to the children

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:35.820
and we are committing to
the youngest children.

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:39.810
So we have a framework
that help us with this.

00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:42.540
We call it the core commitments

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:44.880
for children in humanitarian action.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:48.840
And recently, we have introduced
these three commitments

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.690
for the youngest children in emergencies.

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:54.543
And the three commitments are very simple,

00:25:56.160 --> 00:26:00.030
but they have an incredible potential

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:02.583
to transform societies
in the years to come.

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:05.730
The three commitments refers first

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:08.400
to the services we provide to children.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.950
Quality services for the youngest children

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:13.500
in emergency are our priority.

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:16.980
Children have not only to serve,

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:21.810
but they have also the right
to receive those services.

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:23.820
When I started working in
the humanitarian world,

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:26.520
like 30 years ago, I
have to say something.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:28.440
I was not very committed
to the youngest children

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:31.860
because I was quite unaware of the science

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:34.560
that is behind that I
was explaining before.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:37.357
And I was thinking as a
humanitarian worker, I was thinking,

00:26:37.357 --> 00:26:39.090
"Well, I have to concentrate

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:42.360
in the ones who are suffering the most

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:45.990
and see where we have the biggest impact."

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:50.160
Now I know that those are
actually the youngest children

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:52.080
because the youngest
children don't say, you know,

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:53.797
the children who are one
or two, they don't say,

00:26:53.797 --> 00:26:58.080
"Oh my goodness me, my early
development is being derailing.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:26:59.640
I need help."

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.350
They don't need to say
that because we know it.

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:03.150
We have to provide those services to them,

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:07.110
and services include obviously
health and nutrition,

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:10.540
which is essential up to
75% of nutrients in the body

00:27:11.554 --> 00:27:13.920
in the first 1,000 years of life,

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:18.920
go to nourish only one
organ in the body, the brain

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:24.993
'cause it has those
incredible activity, right,

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:29.160
that I mentioned before,
all these new connectivity.

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:33.270
So nutritious good food,
which is not always easy

00:27:33.270 --> 00:27:36.060
in countries where they are suffering

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:40.020
from conflict or where there is situations

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:41.340
of natural disaster.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:42.590
There are many right now.

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:48.650
Health, obviously,
vaccination very basic things,

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:52.080
hygiene, clean hygiene.

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:55.620
We know what E. coli and the
problems of bad hygiene create

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:57.180
in the development of a child.

00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:59.820
Protection for the children obviously,

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:02.010
and opportunities for
learning also, right,

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:05.010
these opportunities for
engaging in stimulation.

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:09.240
Having also responsive
caregiving is essential.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.540
So all these services and
quality services are fundamental

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:13.710
for the younger children.

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:16.380
The second obviously is
to support the parents

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:18.840
and the caregivers, to support the mothers

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:22.050
but also the fathers who
have to play a huge role

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:23.970
in the upbringing of children

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:26.040
and the development of the brains

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:28.950
of the youngest ones and caregivers.

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:32.820
And this one refers
also to their wellbeing

00:28:32.820 --> 00:28:33.930
and their mental health.

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:37.410
Particularly in situations of emergency,

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:42.410
we are extremely concerned
about the mental health,

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:46.980
the stress and the stressors
that are around families

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:49.290
in situations of emergency,

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:52.080
and finally the capacity
of the frontline workers.

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:55.440
So it's not only the caregivers,
but it's also our doctors,

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:58.770
our nurses, our nutritionists,
our social workers,

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:02.520
our teachers, those
who have direct contact

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:04.380
with children who need

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:07.950
to understand what early
childhood development is,

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:10.392
that they need to participate positively

00:29:10.392 --> 00:29:12.780
in that development.

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:16.320
And with that, those three
commitments and three messages,

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:21.320
I thank you all and I am happy
to give back the floor to Jim

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:25.440
and answer any question that, well,

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:26.790
at least to try to answer some

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:28.470
of the questions you are going to ask.

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:29.706
Thank you.

00:29:29.706 --> 00:29:32.873
(audience applauding)

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:36.720
- It's a pleasure, Eduardo,

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:39.330
to hear your presentations
and to be thoughtful

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:42.150
about the work that you're
doing around the world.

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:44.910
And I think you did mention
something about the number

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:47.160
of children that are affected by conflict

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:48.960
and other realities,

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:53.250
and it's really just heartbreaking,
the number of children

00:29:53.250 --> 00:29:56.100
that are out there that are
facing these challenges.

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:58.950
So thank you very much
for your presentation.

00:29:58.950 --> 00:30:01.410
And you also mentioned some of the things

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:02.880
that are currently ongoing

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.640
and UNICEF certainly has
been leading the way,

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:08.820
along with a number of other
international organizations

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:10.980
to try and address the
needs of these children.

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:13.200
And when you think about the realities

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:14.700
in Ukraine and other places

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:16.800
where there's child displacement,

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:19.110
there's separation of family members,

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:20.299
it's really just tragic

00:30:20.299 --> 00:30:23.101
and so much more needs to be done.

00:30:23.101 --> 00:30:23.934
So I think with that,

00:30:23.934 --> 00:30:27.750
I'll open it up for questions
from people in the audience.

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:29.883
Yes. Maria.

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:33.426
- [Maria] So thank you for
the wonderful presentation.

00:30:33.426 --> 00:30:35.616
I really appreciate it.

00:30:35.616 --> 00:30:37.139
Enjoyed the whole presentation

00:30:37.139 --> 00:30:39.810
and the scientific approach for it.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:42.390
One thing that I was
always wondering about,

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:46.530
I mean the action in emergencies,

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:51.530
the approach from UNICEF is
that given the number of kids,

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:55.080
young kids, that they
might suffer throughout,

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:58.530
I mean ages in different
parts of the globe,

00:30:58.530 --> 00:31:03.530
so how UNICEF would prioritize,
I mean, different regions?

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:07.177
I mean, how does the policy
allow you to think about,

00:31:07.177 --> 00:31:10.140
"Okay, we have 10
emergencies around the globe

00:31:10.140 --> 00:31:13.350
so which one should get the
most, I mean, resources?"

00:31:13.350 --> 00:31:15.960
Because I suspect that
the resources are limited.

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:19.260
I mean, we don't have endless
resources. I appreciate it.

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:20.370
- Yeah, thank you very much.

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:22.920
I don't know if people
online, all the millions

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:26.100
of people who are following
online have heard the question,

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:28.800
but it's a great question.

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:30.573
How do we prioritize, right?

00:31:34.123 --> 00:31:36.867
In a world where there
is so many challenges,

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:41.530
how do we select one
country or the other or how

00:31:42.854 --> 00:31:45.420
to help this community or the other?

00:31:45.420 --> 00:31:46.380
The reality is,

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:50.220
and maybe talking also about
the scale of the problem

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:54.300
that maybe I should give a couple of words

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:55.770
on the scale of the problem

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:58.170
to address then, your question
on how to select, right,

00:31:58.170 --> 00:32:00.720
how to, I dunno if select is the word,

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:02.253
but how to prioritize.

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:05.940
The reality is that every single child,

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:08.820
and I am a pretty optimistic person

00:32:08.820 --> 00:32:12.660
and very positive, I have to say,

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:17.660
so don't get this as a black
cloud now for depressing you,

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:20.550
but the reality is that every single child

00:32:20.550 --> 00:32:25.323
in the world today is affected
at least by one crisis.

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:29.310
And one crisis affects every
single child in the world.

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:31.170
You know which one?

00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:35.610
Climate, climate, thank
you Jim. Climate change.

00:32:35.610 --> 00:32:39.390
And we have had done some
studies from that lately.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:43.511
We did a report that every single child

00:32:43.511 --> 00:32:46.950
of the world is affected
by climate change,

00:32:46.950 --> 00:32:51.510
which can affect the development
in one way or the other

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:55.740
from lead to heat shocks
to the development

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:58.260
of every single child in the world.

00:32:58.260 --> 00:33:00.300
So that's pretty haunting, right?

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:04.287
If we think about two
of the major indicators

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:09.840
of poor child development,

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:14.460
which I will say is child poverty
and poor nutrition status,

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:17.340
just if we look at child poverty,

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:20.160
we are looking around more
than 200 million children

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:25.160
that we have to target in emergencies,

00:33:28.258 --> 00:33:30.690
and so on and so forth.

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:32.400
I mean, there is really hundreds of them.

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:33.360
I can tell you, for example,

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:36.690
an example in Pakistan when
the floats these last year,

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:40.530
the unprecedented floods

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:44.807
in Pakistan affected 36 million people.

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:49.053
I can tell you, the reality is,

00:33:51.500 --> 00:33:54.180
there are no enough resources in the world

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:59.220
to address the magnitude
of these problems.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:00.930
In emergencies usually,

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.200
in my team we say we
do what we can, right?

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:06.780
We look for opportunities,
but it's not only financial.

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:08.610
Financial is just one of them.

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:12.540
We look at financial resources,
we look at human resources,

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:15.690
we look at the community resources,

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:18.420
we look at natural resources,

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:20.760
we look at any resources that we can

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:24.660
to address, depending, you know,
ad hoc and different cases.

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:27.630
But there is also very
good news in that sense

00:34:27.630 --> 00:34:31.140
because we have incredible partnerships

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:33.660
that are really helping with this.

00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:35.250
We have the children themselves

00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:37.080
and their family themselves are the best

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:40.980
and the main partners to
address child development.

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:43.980
Obviously, they're the ones
who do 90% of the development,

00:34:43.980 --> 00:34:46.660
I will say, of the youngest children.

00:34:47.544 --> 00:34:50.280
And we have to work with
them as real partners.

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:52.560
We have to go and in that case,

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:56.040
UNICEF has a little
bit of the UN agencies.

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:57.870
We have an advantage there

00:34:57.870 --> 00:35:00.690
because we go hand in hand with everyone,

00:35:00.690 --> 00:35:03.450
including the governments and authorities

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:06.390
that also have a fundamental role to play.

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:11.390
It's a puzzle of partnerships, of hands

00:35:12.750 --> 00:35:15.000
that are going to help the children,

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:16.740
with the children and the families,

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:19.473
with the families to address all those.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.913
And yes, also one little
important point there.

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:27.360
When there is emergencies,
when there is conflict,

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:28.950
we do what we can.

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:32.130
Conflict by definition is the opposite

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:36.060
of a conducive environment
for development.

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:39.300
So, buffer those environments

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:44.220
to create better
environments for development.

00:35:44.220 --> 00:35:48.630
That's what we do and we do
as much as we can. (laughs)

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:49.830
Thank you.

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:50.830
- Well, thank you

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:53.130
and I think we're running
a little bit short of time,

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:55.530
but Angie, I think there's time at least

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:58.710
for one question coming
from the virtual community.

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:01.443
Is there anything that
comes that you've seen?

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:04.013
- [Angie] There is.

00:36:04.013 --> 00:36:06.810
There is one question from Hinako,

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:09.550
and feel free to open
up your mic and share it

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:12.360
if there's enough time.

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:15.870
If not, I've said that we
will pass it on to Eduardo

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:19.170
and then reply to it in
written communication

00:36:19.170 --> 00:36:21.810
as a briefing to the presentations.

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:23.430
- Well, why don't you go ahead

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:25.260
and have the person pose the question

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:28.713
and I'm sure that Eduardo
will be able to answer.

00:36:29.578 --> 00:36:30.411
- [Angie] Okay.

00:36:30.411 --> 00:36:32.760
Hinako, would you like
me to read it for you?

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:35.880
Or please feel free to open up your mic.

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:37.860
- [Hinako] Yeah, I'm happy to ask.

00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:40.167
Hello, my name is Hinako

00:36:40.167 --> 00:36:42.990
and I'm currently a graduate student

00:36:42.990 --> 00:36:45.030
at the Harvard Graduate
School of Education

00:36:45.030 --> 00:36:48.480
and a mentee of ambassador
Anwarul Chowdhury.

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:51.360
And so my question is,

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:54.570
a lot of my course's
conversation was about sort

00:36:54.570 --> 00:36:57.330
of the protective factors of
early childhood development,

00:36:57.330 --> 00:37:00.570
sort of adults thinking
about how to protect children

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:03.390
but I'm really curious
because I think it's so unique

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:04.470
and powerful to think

00:37:04.470 --> 00:37:09.060
about young children themselves
being the agents of peace.

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:12.573
And so, I would sort of love to hear,

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:17.340
what skills should we be
focusing on fostering.

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:21.690
What are the developmentally
appropriate ways to recognize

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:25.230
and nurture the sense of agency

00:37:25.230 --> 00:37:27.213
for peace among young children?

00:37:28.549 --> 00:37:29.382
Yeah.

00:37:31.128 --> 00:37:34.620
- Hinako, thanks. What a great question.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:35.453
Thank you very much.

00:37:35.453 --> 00:37:37.560
I mean, when you mentioned young children,

00:37:37.560 --> 00:37:41.163
you refer to adolescents, if I am correct?

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:45.023
- [Hinako] I was thinking
more like early childhood.

00:37:45.023 --> 00:37:46.538
- Oh wow, okay.

00:37:46.538 --> 00:37:47.610
- [Hinako] Yeah.

00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:49.230
- Yeah, yeah. Oh, that's a good question.

00:37:49.230 --> 00:37:53.943
I'm going to address or try
to address a little bit both.

00:37:55.828 --> 00:38:00.828
The youngest children are
not only victims of crisis.

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:07.970
They are also the biggest
asset to address crisis.

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:12.840
And through the investments in
early childhood development,

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:15.120
we are looking at early
childhood development

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:20.120
and the best ways to
construct cohesive societies,

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:23.460
not only for the future,

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:26.103
but in the moment of
the emergencies, also.

00:38:28.428 --> 00:38:29.820
One of the good things I have to say

00:38:29.820 --> 00:38:33.930
in emergency is that
the youngest children,

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:38.930
the children under eight are
not controversial in many ways.

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:42.600
Well, everything can be controversial,

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:43.433
but I will say it's one

00:38:43.433 --> 00:38:45.900
of the least controversial things, right?

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:49.620
To feed and to support children,

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:52.290
the younger children is going to be one

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:55.140
of the driving factors for social cohesion

00:38:55.140 --> 00:39:00.140
and for addressing emergencies.

00:39:01.650 --> 00:39:04.350
The children, the youngest children.

00:39:04.350 --> 00:39:07.890
- [Jim] We may be asking
you to respond also

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:11.490
and you can send a message
to Angie for the remainder

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:12.427
because we're running a little short.

00:39:12.427 --> 00:39:14.070
- Yeah, and we want Siobhan.

00:39:14.070 --> 00:39:16.560
Let's do that, and then
we take this further

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:20.400
because also, we can talk about
the younger and bit older,

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.050
the adolescents being engaged

00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:23.880
on supporting early childhood development

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:26.430
as the best advocates to do so.

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:27.263
Thanks, Jim.

00:39:27.263 --> 00:39:28.863
- Thank you. Muchos Gracias.

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:34.650
So I'm gonna turn the
floor over to Siobhan,

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:37.470
but it's also fascinating
because one of the things

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:39.300
that she's been working on,

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:42.150
and I'm not sure if Sherry
Westin's in the audience or not,

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:45.030
but when you think about "Sesame
Street" and the rest of it,

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:47.670
but she's gonna be actually
sharing with us some

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:50.370
of the things that she's been
doing in Northern Ireland

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:54.120
and in Ireland in general
to address this issue.

00:39:54.120 --> 00:39:57.210
So I think I will move ahead with this.

00:39:57.210 --> 00:40:01.260
And Siobhan is actually
the current chairperson

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:03.390
of the International
Network on Peace Building

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:04.890
for Young Children,

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:07.830
and she's also the former
chief executive officer

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:11.457
of Early Years, the
Organization for Young Children.

00:40:11.457 --> 00:40:13.860
And Early Years is a
non-profit organization

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:16.917
that's been working since
1965 to promote peace

00:40:16.917 --> 00:40:20.307
and high quality childcare for children

00:40:20.307 --> 00:40:23.100
and their families based
in Northern Ireland.

00:40:23.100 --> 00:40:24.210
So I'm not sure, Siobhan,

00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:26.280
if you're actually able
to advance the slides

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:28.050
but I can do that if you need me to.

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:31.500
- [Siobhan] Yeah, I think you're
going to do it for me, Jim.

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:32.520
- Pardon me?

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:34.410
- [Siobhan] I think you're
going to do it for me.

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:35.790
I think that's what I was told,

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:37.710
that you would advance the slides.

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:41.340
Many thanks. Good afternoon,
good morning everyone.

00:40:41.340 --> 00:40:43.140
Greetings from Northern Ireland.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:46.860
I'm sorry I can't be with
you there in the auditorium,

00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:50.610
but perhaps sometime
during the lecture series.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:54.990
In this session, I hope
to describe to you how we

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:59.550
as an organization pioneered
a culture of respect

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:02.010
for difference with young children

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:05.370
and hopefully, I'll be able to
answer some of the questions

00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:08.310
that have been raised so far.

00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:10.380
Our work was not only with young children,

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:13.620
but also with their teachers and parents

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:18.620
against a backdrop of a
deeply divided society scarred

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:22.890
and traumatized by 30 years of conflict.

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:26.370
I will describe to you how
we developed a new program,

00:41:26.370 --> 00:41:29.610
the Media Initiative
Respecting Difference Program,

00:41:29.610 --> 00:41:33.150
and implemented this
across Northern Ireland,

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:34.830
Ireland and beyond

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:39.830
and also subjected it to
a variety of evaluations,

00:41:40.020 --> 00:41:43.863
including a large random control trial.

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:49.830
As Jim has said, the
organization was founded in 1965,

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:53.010
just a few short years before the outbreak

00:41:53.010 --> 00:41:56.371
of conflict in 1969.

00:41:56.371 --> 00:41:58.680
And it was always committed

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:03.680
to creating a cross community,
non-sectarian approach,

00:42:04.140 --> 00:42:07.980
ensuring that the two
main traditions then,

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:11.970
Catholics and Protestants,
Republican Nationalist,

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:15.990
Loyalist Unionist families
could come together

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:18.180
where parents could get to know each other

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:21.603
and children could begin to play together.

00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:24.090
Against the backdrop

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:26.970
of what we euphemistically
call The Troubles,

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:31.970
The Northern Ireland
Conflict 1969 to 1994,

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:36.930
these services for young
children provided a safe space

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:40.620
for parents across the religious divide

00:42:40.620 --> 00:42:44.700
and provided opportunities
to support one another

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:47.943
through the horror that
was unfolding around them.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:51.900
After the cease fires in 1994 and

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:54.137
with investment by the European Union

00:42:54.137 --> 00:42:59.010
in the special support program
for peace and reconciliation,

00:42:59.010 --> 00:43:03.240
investment in early
childhood services was seen

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:07.170
and still is seen as
one of the key vehicles

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:09.333
for building the peace
in Northern Ireland.

00:43:10.350 --> 00:43:12.390
The signing of the Good Friday Agreement

00:43:12.390 --> 00:43:16.410
in 1998 was a significant
step forward in terms

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:20.340
of taking the gun out of Irish politics.

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:23.520
The Good Friday agreement
with a focus on rights,

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:28.520
social justice and reconciliation
presented a very positive,

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:30.900
policy environment for the development

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:33.153
of services for young children.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:39.030
Given our long history of
supporting high quality,

00:43:39.030 --> 00:43:43.710
non-sectarian, evidence-informed
early year services,

00:43:43.710 --> 00:43:48.710
we were confident that early
childhood services could play,

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:51.480
an ever increasing role in the quest

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:54.630
for social cohesion and peace building.

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:58.740
Thank you, Jim, for
sharing this next slide

00:43:58.740 --> 00:44:03.240
because the publication
of this research in 2001,

00:44:04.507 --> 00:44:09.090
"Too Young to Notice?"
 was a wake up call

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:12.630
for us all in the early childhood sector.

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:17.340
The research presented the
harsh reality that children

00:44:17.340 --> 00:44:20.160
as young as three in Northern Ireland,

00:44:20.160 --> 00:44:24.630
while developing positive
aspects of their own identity,

00:44:24.630 --> 00:44:27.570
were however developing negative,

00:44:27.570 --> 00:44:30.990
prejudicial attitudes to others.

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:34.200
And as you can see, by the age of six,

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:36.903
these attitudes were becoming embedded.

00:44:37.860 --> 00:44:42.860
The research also challenged
a prevailing view at the time

00:44:43.110 --> 00:44:45.510
that young children were too young

00:44:45.510 --> 00:44:49.593
to notice the sectarian
divisions that surrounded them.

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:55.560
This research was a catalyst
for us in the organization

00:44:55.560 --> 00:44:59.820
to rethink our approach to
dealing with issues of race,

00:44:59.820 --> 00:45:02.553
religion, ethnicity and identity.

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:08.600
And we took an explicit,
intentional approach to move

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:12.780
from being a non-sectarian organization

00:45:12.780 --> 00:45:16.263
to becoming an
anti-sectarian organization.

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:20.490
We were supported with a range

00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:24.960
of multi-level stakeholder
partnerships including media,

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:29.640
academia, education
partners, policy makers

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:34.640
to work collaboratively to
develop a new Early Years program

00:45:34.650 --> 00:45:37.320
that became known as the Media Initiative.

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:39.063
Next slide please, Jim.

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:45.060
The program aimed to develop a
respecting difference culture

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:47.700
that celebrated the variety of differences

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:50.280
in Northern Ireland society.

00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:54.110
And while ethnic and
religious differences had been

00:45:54.110 --> 00:45:56.130
at the heart of our conflict,

00:45:56.130 --> 00:45:57.810
there was a recognition of the need

00:45:57.810 --> 00:46:00.750
to celebrate other emerging differences

00:46:00.750 --> 00:46:04.620
on the basis of race, disability, gender,

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:09.620
and also promote an
anti-bullying, respectful culture

00:46:09.930 --> 00:46:14.340
that would underpin relationships
between children, parents,

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:17.430
teachers, and the wider community.

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:21.360
The program had a number
of distinct new components,

00:46:21.360 --> 00:46:24.390
the development of five
persona characters,

00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:27.480
representing religious, physical, ethnic,

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:29.370
and race differences,

00:46:29.370 --> 00:46:34.370
the development of five
cartoon television messages,

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:38.730
reflecting instances of
exclusion and inclusion.

00:46:38.730 --> 00:46:40.740
These messages were initially played

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:44.580
on national television
regularly throughout the year

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:46.530
and were watched by children,

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:49.140
parents, and the wider community.

00:46:49.140 --> 00:46:51.150
The development of a new curriculum

00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:54.060
and service design manual aimed

00:46:54.060 --> 00:46:58.740
at supporting preschool teachers
implement this new approach

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:02.580
in a culturally and
contextually appropriate manner,

00:47:02.580 --> 00:47:06.480
new training for preschool
teachers and parents based

00:47:06.480 --> 00:47:09.900
on reflective techniques
that helped parents

00:47:09.900 --> 00:47:14.370
and teachers reflect on their
own experience of difference

00:47:14.370 --> 00:47:18.780
and supporting them to
understand and respect the other.

00:47:18.780 --> 00:47:21.570
The development of new resources reflect

00:47:21.570 --> 00:47:24.030
in the religious, cultural,

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:29.030
sporting and musical traditions
within a divided society,

00:47:29.160 --> 00:47:33.600
capacity building for management
committees, school leaders

00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:36.420
so that they could support the development

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:39.870
of anti-sectarian policies and strategies

00:47:39.870 --> 00:47:43.563
within their local services
and their local communities.

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:48.000
This new program was influenced
by the need to move away

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:50.820
from a superficial approach to dealing

00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:54.180
with religious, race and ethnic issues.

00:47:54.180 --> 00:47:56.520
Traditionally in Northern Ireland,

00:47:56.520 --> 00:48:00.300
early childhood programs had
adopted a neutral approach

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:05.160
to such issues, which in fact
meant they were often ignored.

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:07.830
We wanted to move away from approaches

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:11.010
that supported limited
contact for children

00:48:11.010 --> 00:48:12.570
from different religious,

00:48:12.570 --> 00:48:15.900
ethnic and socioeconomic backgrounds

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:19.410
and were keen to create deep, meaningful,

00:48:19.410 --> 00:48:22.260
prolonged daily experiences

00:48:22.260 --> 00:48:25.350
for children, families, and teachers.

00:48:25.350 --> 00:48:28.770
We were influenced by
theorists on social cohesion

00:48:28.770 --> 00:48:32.640
that stressed the importance
of building trust,

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:36.780
respect for each other
and a sense of belonging.

00:48:36.780 --> 00:48:39.210
The program was initially
targeted at three

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:40.500
and four year olds,

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:44.400
but has since been extended
to work with two year olds

00:48:44.400 --> 00:48:47.700
and eight year olds in
full daycare settings

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:50.640
and in afterschool environments.

00:48:50.640 --> 00:48:53.220
We were introducing a new approach,

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:57.150
an approach that took an
intentional, anti-sectarian,

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:00.420
contextually appropriate
focus to the issues

00:49:00.420 --> 00:49:02.730
that had caused enormous division

00:49:02.730 --> 00:49:05.070
within Northern Ireland society.

00:49:05.070 --> 00:49:07.890
Sport, music, cultural activities

00:49:07.890 --> 00:49:11.610
and flags were often symbols of division

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:13.920
across Northern Ireland society.

00:49:13.920 --> 00:49:18.660
And yet, this new approach
intentionally use these symbols

00:49:18.660 --> 00:49:21.480
of division to create a culture

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:25.080
of respect and understanding difference.

00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:28.980
It was vitally important,
therefore, that evidence ensured

00:49:28.980 --> 00:49:32.700
that the approach was
not further exacerbating

00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:36.930
at the situation and creating
even further divisions.

00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:39.270
Next slide please, Jim.

00:49:39.270 --> 00:49:43.200
Between 2003 and 2010,

00:49:43.200 --> 00:49:47.280
Early Years continued to develop
and implement the program

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:50.460
and with support from
Atlantic Philanthropies,

00:49:50.460 --> 00:49:53.430
we were able to commission
Queen's University

00:49:53.430 --> 00:49:56.040
in Belfast, Stranmillis College

00:49:56.040 --> 00:49:58.560
and the National Children's Bureau

00:49:58.560 --> 00:50:01.800
to carry out a random control trial,

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:04.560
involving 80 preschool settings

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:08.100
in both Northern Ireland
and the Republic of Ireland

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:09.390
and this was coupled

00:50:09.390 --> 00:50:11.853
with a number of qualitative case studies.

00:50:13.110 --> 00:50:15.900
The results were a positive indication

00:50:15.900 --> 00:50:19.830
that the Media Initiative was
having a significant impact

00:50:19.830 --> 00:50:22.470
in supporting young children's social

00:50:22.470 --> 00:50:26.130
and emotional development
and creating a culture

00:50:26.130 --> 00:50:29.460
of inclusion and respecting difference.

00:50:29.460 --> 00:50:32.880
The positive results from
the random control trial

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:36.960
and the qualitative care
studies brought both political

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:41.070
and founder attention to
the innovative approach.

00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:45.300
And over the next decade,
investment for implementation

00:50:45.300 --> 00:50:50.130
and going to scale was provided
by a number of sources.

00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:54.120
Since 2010 until now,

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:57.360
Early Years has developed
a systematic approach

00:50:57.360 --> 00:51:01.710
to both scaling up and implementation.

00:51:01.710 --> 00:51:06.480
The approach has involved
developing a scaling up strategy,

00:51:06.480 --> 00:51:11.480
establishing implementation
teams and detailed advocacy

00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:16.640
and sensitization strategy
to convince case stakeholders

00:51:18.870 --> 00:51:22.740
to support implementation
and provide political,

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:27.330
financial and institutional
sustainability.

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:29.010
Since its inception,

00:51:29.010 --> 00:51:31.910
the program has been
adapted and implemented

00:51:31.910 --> 00:51:35.550
in a range of local and global context.

00:51:35.550 --> 00:51:40.080
The program has been successfully
implemented and evaluated

00:51:40.080 --> 00:51:44.667
in the Western Balkans, in
Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan

00:51:45.570 --> 00:51:48.450
and it is hoped that we
will implement the program

00:51:48.450 --> 00:51:51.453
in Palestine, Israel, and Columbia.

00:51:52.590 --> 00:51:56.820
The Media Initiative has also
been a significant component

00:51:56.820 --> 00:52:00.840
in the new Shared Education
program in Northern Ireland

00:52:00.840 --> 00:52:03.420
and the Republic of
Ireland through the Sharing

00:52:03.420 --> 00:52:07.590
from the Start project,
funded by the European Union.

00:52:07.590 --> 00:52:11.070
And this program aims to
provide young children

00:52:11.070 --> 00:52:14.700
with even greater
educational opportunities

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:19.700
to be educated together,
rather in the segregated system

00:52:20.370 --> 00:52:22.743
that has been the system to date.

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:27.893
In subsequent lectures, I hope
to share with you the details

00:52:27.893 --> 00:52:29.760
on the International Network

00:52:29.760 --> 00:52:32.460
on Peace Building and perspectives

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:35.760
from key global partners who have adapted

00:52:35.760 --> 00:52:37.920
and implemented the model

00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:40.590
that has been developed
in Northern Ireland.

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:41.423
Thank you.

